Windows: make runtime-venv creation robust to MAX_PATH and symlink refusal.

Under `bootstrap_impl=script` with `venvs_use_declare_symlink=no`, the
stage-2 bootstrap recreates the binary's venv at runtime (in $TMP or
RULES_PYTHON_EXTRACT_ROOT) and populates it by symlinking the
interpreter, bin entries, and the site-packages tree out of runfiles
(`_create_venv` / `_symlink_tree` in python_bootstrap_template.txt).

Two Windows failure modes are handled:

1. MAX_PATH. Win32 path-based APIs reject paths longer than 260 chars
   unless the host enables the LongPathsEnabled registry setting, which
   cannot be relied upon. Deep runfiles venv trees regularly exceed the
   limit — observed in mongodb CI with a 265-char source path (while
   its 245-char parent directory still listed fine via os.walk):

     os.symlink(...)   -> OSError: [WinError 87] The parameter is incorrect
     shutil.copy2(...) -> FileNotFoundError: [WinError 3] The system
                          cannot find the path specified  (CopyFile2)

   Fix: route both ends of every symlink/copy through the \\?\
   extended-length prefix (\\?\UNC\ for UNC paths) when the path
   exceeds the limit.

2. Symlink refusal. Symlink creation requires
   SeCreateSymbolicLinkPrivilege or Developer Mode; hosts without it
   fail with ERROR_PRIVILEGE_NOT_HELD (WinError 1314) or
   ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER (WinError 87). Fix: fall back to copying
   (shutil.copy2 / copytree) — the same degradation strategy CPython's
   own venv module uses on Windows. The runtime venv is ephemeral or
   per-binary cached, so a copy is functionally equivalent to a
   symlink, just slower to create.

Non-Windows behavior is unchanged (OSError re-raised, no path munging).

Applied via `single_version_override(patches=...)` on rules_python in
//MODULE.bazel. Candidate for upstreaming to bazel-contrib/rules_python.

--- a/python/private/python_bootstrap_template.txt	2026-07-23 10:54:27
+++ b/python/private/python_bootstrap_template.txt	2026-07-23 14:29:06
@@ -558,8 +558,31 @@
     with open(python_program, 'rb') as f:
       print_verbose("pyprog head:" + str(f.read(50)))
     raise
+
+def _win_extended_path(p):
+  r"""Return an extended-length (\\?\) form of an absolute Windows path.
+
+  Win32 path-based APIs (CreateSymbolicLinkW, CopyFile2, ...) reject paths
+  longer than MAX_PATH (260) unless the host opts in via the registry
+  LongPathsEnabled setting, which can't be relied upon. Deep runfiles
+  venv trees regularly exceed 260 chars; the failures surface as
+  ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER (WinError 87) from CreateSymbolicLinkW and
+  ERROR_PATH_NOT_FOUND (WinError 3) from CopyFile2. The \\?\ prefix
+  bypasses MAX_PATH regardless of host configuration. Only applied when
+  the path actually exceeds the limit, to minimize behavior change.
+  """
+  p = abspath(p)
+  if len(p) < 260 or p.startswith("\\\\?\\"):
+    return p
+  if p.startswith("\\\\"):
+    return "\\\\?\\UNC\\" + p[2:]
+  return "\\\\?\\" + p
 
+
 def _symlink_exist_ok(*, from_, to):
+  if IS_WINDOWS:
+    from_ = _win_extended_path(from_)
+    to = _win_extended_path(to)
   try:
     # On Windows, symlinks have to be told whether they're
     # pointing to a file or directory. Python is supposed to auto-detect
@@ -567,6 +590,22 @@
     os.symlink(to, from_, target_is_directory=os.path.isdir(to))
   except FileExistsError:
     pass
+  except OSError:
+    if not IS_WINDOWS:
+      raise
+    # Windows hosts may also refuse symlink creation outright: it
+    # requires SeCreateSymbolicLinkPrivilege or Developer Mode, and the
+    # refusal surfaces as ERROR_PRIVILEGE_NOT_HELD (WinError 1314) or
+    # ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER (WinError 87, when the unprivileged-create
+    # flag isn't supported). Fall back to copying — the same strategy
+    # CPython's venv module uses on Windows. The runtime-recreated venv
+    # is ephemeral (or cached under RULES_PYTHON_EXTRACT_ROOT), so the
+    # copy is functionally equivalent to a symlink, just slower to
+    # create.
+    if os.path.isdir(to):
+      shutil.copytree(to, from_, dirs_exist_ok=True)
+    else:
+      shutil.copy2(to, from_)
 
 
